1
00:00:00,730 --> 00:00:05,075
Today we're pleased to have on the show Alice Bussell from the Dolphin Conservation

2
00:00:05,075 --> 00:00:05,497
Trust.

3
00:00:05,497 --> 00:00:07,690
Tell us about the Trust, Alice.

4
00:00:07,690 --> 00:00:13,810
Well, obviously its purpose is to protect dolphins in seas all around the world.

5
00:00:13,810 --> 00:00:18,864
It tries to raise people's awareness of the problems these marine creatures are suffering

6
00:00:18,864 --> 00:00:21,030
because of pollution and other threats.

7
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:26,033
It started ten years ago and it's one of the fastest growing animal charities

8
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:30,678
in the country - although it's still fairly small compared with the big players

9
00:00:30,678 --> 00:00:31,750
in animal protection.

10
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:36,433
We are particularly proud of the work we do in education 一 last year

11
00:00:36,433 --> 00:00:43,278
we visited a huge number of schools in different parts of the country, going round to talk to children

12
00:00:43,278 --> 00:00:46,160
and young people aged from five to eighteen.

13
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:50,340
In fact, about thirty-five per cent of our members are children.

14
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:57,473
The charity uses its money to support campaigns -for example, for changes in fishing policy

15
00:00:57,473 --> 00:00:58,900
and so forth.

16
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:04,324
It hopes soon to be able to employ its first full-time biologist - with

17
00:01:04,324 --> 00:01:06,410
dolphin expertise - to monitor populations.

18
00:01:06,410 --> 00:01:10,998
Of course, many people give their services on a voluntary basis

19
00:01:10,998 --> 00:01:16,420
and we now have volunteers working in observation, office work and other things.

20
00:01:16,420 --> 00:01:21,360
I should also tell you about the award we won from the Charity Commission

21
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:23,830
last year - for our work in education.

22
00:01:23,830 --> 00:01:28,584
Although it's not meant an enormous amount of money for us, it has made

23
00:01:28,584 --> 00:01:31,300
our activities even more widely publicised and understood.

24
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:34,918
In the long term it may not bring in extra members

25
00:01:34,918 --> 00:01:37,220
but we're hoping it'll have this effect.

26
00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:41,140
Is it possible to see dolphins in UK waters?

27
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:41,190
Yes.

28
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:42,665
In several locations.

29
00:01:42,665 --> 00:01:47,240
And we have a big project in the east part of Scotland.

30
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:51,700
This has long been a haven for dolphins because it has very little shipping.

31
00:01:51,700 --> 00:01:52,110
However,

32
00:01:52,110 --> 00:01:57,850
that may be about to change soon because oil companies want to increase exploration

33
00:01:57,850 --> 00:01:58,260
there.

34
00:01:58,260 --> 00:02:04,722
We're campaigning against this because, although there'll be little pollution from oil, exploration creates

35
00:02:04,722 --> 00:02:07,030
a lot of underwater noise.

36
00:02:07,030 --> 00:02:10,360
It means the dolphins can't rest and socialise.

37
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:15,150
This is how I became interested in dolphin conservation in the first place.

38
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:19,593
I had never seen one and I hadn't been particularly interested in them at

39
00:02:19,593 --> 00:02:19,910
school.

40
00:02:19,910 --> 00:02:24,458
Then I came across this story about a family of dolphins who had to

41
00:02:24,458 --> 00:02:28,356
leave their home in the Moray Firth because of the oil companies

42
00:02:28,356 --> 00:02:31,280
and about a child who campaigned to save them.

43
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:35,990
I couldn't put the book down - I was hooked.

44
00:02:35,990 --> 00:02:39,611
I'm sure our listeners will want to find out what they can do to

45
00:02:39,611 --> 00:02:39,870
help.

46
00:02:39,870 --> 00:02:42,390
You mentioned the 'Adopt a Dolphin' scheme.

47
00:02:42,390 --> 00:02:44,550
Can you tell us about that?

48
00:02:44,550 --> 00:02:45,366
Of course!

49
00:02:45,366 --> 00:02:49,040
People can choose one of our dolphins to sponsor.

50
00:02:49,040 --> 00:02:52,470
They receive a picture of it and news updates.

51
00:02:52,470 --> 00:02:58,847
I'd like to tell you about four which are currently being adopted by our

52
00:02:58,847 --> 00:02:59,302
members:

53
00:02:59,302 --> 00:03:01,580
Moondancer, Echo, Kiwi and Samson.

54
00:03:01,580 --> 00:03:07,611
Unfortunately, Echo is being rather elusive this year and hasn't yet been sighted by our observers

55
00:03:07,611 --> 00:03:11,380
but we remain optimistic that he'll be out there soon.

56
00:03:11,380 --> 00:03:16,456
All the others have been out in force - Samson and Moondancer are often photographed together

57
00:03:16,456 --> 00:03:22,885
but it is Kiwi who's our real character as she seems to love coming up close for the cameras

58
00:03:22,885 --> 00:03:25,930
and we've captured her on film hundreds of times.

59
00:03:25,930 --> 00:03:30,596
They all have their own personalities - Moondancer is very elegant and curves out

60
00:03:30,596 --> 00:03:35,261
and into the water very smoothly, whereas Samson has a lot of energy -

61
00:03:35,261 --> 00:03:38,850
he's always leaping out of the water with great vigour.

62
00:03:38,850 --> 00:03:43,815
You'd probably expect him to be the youngest - he's not quite - that's Kiwi -

63
00:03:43,815 --> 00:03:48,780
but Samson's the latest of our dolphins to be chosen for the scheme.

64
00:03:48,780 --> 00:03:53,310
Kiwi makes a lot of noise so we can often pick her out straightaway.

65
00:03:53,310 --> 00:03:55,392
Echo and Moondancer are noisy too,

66
00:03:55,392 --> 00:04:00,251
but Moondancer's easy to find because she has a particularly large fin on her

67
00:04:00,251 --> 00:04:00,598
back,

68
00:04:00,598 --> 00:04:02,680
which makes her easy to identify.

69
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:05,220
So, yes, they're all very different...

70
00:04:05,220 --> 00:04:07,560
Well, they sound a fascinating group ...
